We have class Stack<E> extends Vector<E>. push adds element using addElement(item) (Vector method). Need to assert: If the pushed item is null, the last element in the stack is also null; otherwise, the last element in the stack is equal to the pushed item.

We need to refer to last element in the stack after operation. Since Stack extends Vector, we can use methods of Vector: maybe elementAt(int index) or lastElement()? In java.util.Vector, there is method lastElement() returning last element, and elementAt(index). Since we must use publicly accessible observer methods provided in the test function. In this class, we have size(), maybe also elementAt? Not defined here, but inherited from Vector, which is public. However spec says use only publicly accessible observer methods provided in the test function. The test function is push, which returns item. We can use this.lastElement()? It's a method of Vector, public E lastElement(). So we can use this.lastElement().

We need to compare with item. Since item may be null, equality should be (item == null ? this.lastElement() == null : item.equals(this.lastElement()))? But spec uses "equal to" meaning equals method. For null case, we just compare null. So we can write: (item == null) => this.lastElement() == null && (item != null) => this.lastElement().equals(item). However need to use => syntax with parentheses.

Thus assert: (item == null) => this.lastElement() == null && (item != null) => this.lastElement().equals(item);

But need parentheses for grouping due to => lower precedence than &&. So we do: (item == null) => (this.lastElement() == null) && (item != null) => (this.lastElement().equals(item));

But note that the second part uses .equals, which may throw NPE if this.lastElement() is null. However if item != null, then after push, last element should be equal to item, which is not null, so last element should not be null, .equals safe. So fine.

Thus final answer code block.

